# THE GENERATED PRISMA CLIENT IS A DEPLOY ARTIFACT, AND IT IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE.
#
# Do not gitignore public/_prisma/client. It carries libquery_engine-debian-openssl-3.0.x.so.node — the engine Cloud
# Run loads — and the hosting packager snapshots public/ OUT OF THE SOURCE TREE before the build runs, so a client
# that is not committed is a function with no query engine. That is the "ENGINE NOT FOUND" outage this repo spent
# roughly ten deploys on (see prisma/schema.prisma's generator block, and lib/db/prismaSchemaSync.test.ts).
# scripts/prisma-artifact-tests.ts asserts the tracking, so the mistake fails a suite now instead of a deploy.
#
# What these attributes buy: the artifact is ~43 MB of generated JS plus two engine binaries, and it goes DIRTY on any
# machine whose `native` binaryTarget differs from the one it was generated on (the schema pins
# ["native", "debian-openssl-3.0.x"], so a Mac bakes darwin-arm64 into it and a Linux container regenerates it for its
# own platform). Generator paths are repo-relative: always regenerate with `npm run prisma:generate`
# (scripts/prisma-generate.mjs); a bare `prisma generate` re-bakes this machine's absolute path and dirties the tree.
# Marking it generated keeps the remaining churn out of diffs and reviews. It stays tracked.
public/_prisma/client/** -diff linguist-generated=true
public/_prisma/client/*.node binary
